{
  "_comment": [
    "RESERVED SLOT. A working Unreal MCP bridge already exists on this machine:",
    "UE 5.8+ ships Epic's own ModelContextProtocol plugin, and it is registered with",
    "Claude Code at USER scope, so every session in every directory sees it and connects",
    "automatically when an Unreal editor is running.",
    "",
    "Check it with:   claude mcp list",
    "",
    "DO NOT add a project-scoped .mcp.json in this folder - a duplicate entry shows as",
    "'Pending approval' and shadows the working one.",
    "",
    "Use this file only when:",
    "  (a) the user-scope entry is missing. Restore it with:",
    "        claude mcp add --scope user --transport http unreal-mcp http://127.0.0.1:8000/mcp",
    "  (b) you need a SECOND editor on its own port (every editor defaults to 8000, and a",
    "      second one cannot bind it - the client would silently stay on the first):",
    "        UnrealEditor.exe \"D:\\UE\\<Project>\\<Project>.uproject\" \\",
    "          -ModelContextProtocolPort=8010 -ModelContextProtocolStartServer",
    "        then add a second server entry for that port.",
    "",
    "READ AGENTS.md section 4b and 4c FIRST. Epic's built-in ModelContextProtocol plugin",
    "(port 8000) and the Unreal Canvas / merlin server are DIFFERENT servers that both look",
    "like 'the Unreal MCP'. Verify with:",
    "  Get-NetTCPConnection -LocalPort 8000 -State Listen",
    "and read the _meta 'com.epicgames.unrealengine.instance' block in the initialize",
    "response - it tells you which editor and which project answered.",
    "",
    "The MCP plugin ships with UE 5.8 and newer only. It does NOT exist in UE 5.5.",
    "",
    "The block below is what the plugin's own console command",
    "'ModelContextProtocol.GenerateClientConfig ClaudeCode' writes."
  ],
  "mcpServers": {
    "unreal-mcp": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "http://127.0.0.1:8000/mcp"
    }
  }
}
